# Static Analysis Reviewer

Use this skill when reviewing code changes in Go, Python, Rust, JavaScript, or TypeScript repositories for quality signals: dead code, unused dependencies, unused exports/files, cycles, architecture boundaries, duplication, complexity, and maintainability.

## Review stance

- Prefer language-native static analysis over general style linting.
- Run read-only checks first. Only use autofix commands when the user explicitly asks for cleanup.
- Treat analyzer output as evidence, not as an automatic deletion plan. Dead-code and unused-export tools can produce false positives around dynamic loading, reflection, macros, generated code, plugin entrypoints, public APIs, and cross-platform build tags.
- Report findings with file paths, tool names, and the exact command that produced the evidence.
- Git-dependent checks should run in normal project Git worktrees. In a scratch project without `.git`, record those checks as skipped with `SKIPPED: no git worktree`, run the remaining language checks, and do not fail the review solely because Git metadata is absent.

